</back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Inhibition of the &#x003b1;<sub>1A</sub> Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel by &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA. (A) Inhibition of Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel currents by &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA. Current records elicited by depolarization in either the absence or presence of toxin. Charge carrier was 1 mM Ba<sup>2+</sup>. Leak and capacitive currents have been isolated and subtracted using 100 &#x003bc;M Cd to block the Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel. (B) Time course for onset and recovery for inhibition by &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA. Steady state Ba<sup>2+</sup> current elicited by depolarizations to &#x02212;15 mV at 10-s intervals before and after application of the toxin. Inward Ba<sup>2+</sup> currents were measured 10 ms after depolarization. (C) Fraction of uninhibited current elicited by various strength depolarizations. Steady state Ba<sup>2+</sup> currents were elicited in the presence (I) or absence (I<sub>0</sub>) of toxin using 30-ms depolarizations between &#x02212;25 and +25 mV. Holding voltage was &#x02212;90 mV in all cases. Data are means &#x000b1; SEM for three oocytes at each toxin concentration. (D) Plot of fraction of uninhibited (I<sup>n</sup>/I<sup>n</sup><sub>0</sub>) current against toxin concentration. I<sup>n</sup>/I<sup>n</sup><sub>0</sub> is the fraction of uninhibited current in the plateau phase at negative voltages, typically &#x02013;25 to &#x02013;15 mV (see C). Smooth curve is a fit of I<sup>n</sup>/I<sup>n</sup><sub>0</sub> = 1 &#x02212; {[toxin]/ ([toxin] + <italic>K</italic><sub>d</sub>)} to the data, where <italic>K</italic><sub>d</sub> is the equilibrium dissociation constant for toxin binding to a single site on the channel. Data are from means &#x000b1; SEM for between three and five oocytes at each toxin concentration.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8186.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Sequence comparison between voltage-gated ion channels in the gating modifier toxin binding region. Membrane folding model for a K<sup>+</sup> channel &#x003b1; subunit and sequence alignment between K<sup>+</sup>, Ca<sup>2+</sup>, and Na<sup>+</sup> channels in S3&#x02013;S4. Cylinders demarcate the approximate positions of &#x003b1;-helices based on scanning mutagenesis in both <italic>Shaker</italic> and <italic>drk</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channels (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="Monksetal1999">Monks et al. 1999</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bib" rid="HongandMiller2000">Hong and Miller 2000</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bib" rid="Li-Smerinetal2000">Li-Smerin et al. 2000</xref>). Underlined region of sequence marks approximate transmembrane segments identified by hydrophobicity analysis. The three positions highlighted in yellow correspond to positions in the <italic>drk1</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel (Ile273, Phe274, and Glu277) where mutations alter Hanatoxin affinity (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="SwartzandMacKinnon1997b">Swartz and MacKinnon 1997b</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bib" rid="Li-SmerinandSwartz1998">Li-Smerin and Swartz 1998</xref>, <xref ref-type="bib" rid="Li-SmerinandSwartz2000">Li-Smerin and Swartz 2000</xref>). Mutation of Glu1613 in repeat IV of the Na<sup>+</sup> channel (highlighted yellow) decreases the binding affinity of &#x003b1;-scorpion and sea anemone toxins (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="Rogersetal1996">Rogers et al. 1996</xref>). In the &#x003b1;-<sub>1A</sub> Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel, two repeats contain Glu residues (Glu188 and Glu1658&#x0003b; marked by yellow highlighting and the red box) at positions corresponding to Glu277 in the <italic>drk1</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel and Glu 1613 in the brain IIA Na<sup>+</sup> channel. The black arrow under the sequence for repeat IV in the &#x003b1;-<sub>1A</sub> Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel marks the position where insertion of Asn and Pro has been reported to decrease &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA affinity (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="Bourinetetal1999">Bourinet et al. 1999</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bib" rid="Hansetal1999">Hans et al. 1999</xref>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8186.f1"/></fig><fig id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Gating properties of mutant &#x003b1;<sub>1A</sub> Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels. (A) Steady state current&#x02013;voltage relations for wild-type and two mutant Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels. Ba<sup>2+</sup> currents were measured 10 ms after depolarization and have been normalized to the maximal inward current. Charge carrier was 1 mM Ba<sup>2+</sup>. Leak currents were subtracted using 100 &#x003bc;M Cd. (B) Normalized conductance&#x02013;voltage (G-V) relations for wild-type and two mutants channels. G was calculated according to G = I<sub>Ba2+</sub>/(V &#x02212; V<sub>rev</sub>) with V<sub>rev</sub> = +29 mV. Symbols are means &#x000b1; SEM for four to five cells each. Smooth curves are single Boltzmann fits to the data with the following parameters: (wild-type) z = 6.8, V<sub>50</sub> = &#x02212;18.9 mV&#x0003b; (Glu188Lys) z = 6.0, V<sub>50</sub> = &#x02212;11.9 mV&#x0003b; and (Glu1658Lys) z = 5.8, V<sub>50</sub> = &#x02212;10.9 mV.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8186.f3a"/><graphic xlink:href="JGP8186.f3b"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Altered interaction of &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA with mutant Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels. (A) Current records elicited by depolarization in the absence and presence of 1 &#x003bc;M &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA. Depolarizations were elicited to &#x02212;15 mV for wild type and to &#x02212;10 mV for both Glu188Lys and Glu1658Lys. Holding voltage was &#x02212;90 mV. Charge carrier was 1 mM Ba<sup>2+</sup>. Leak and capacitive currents have been isolated and subtracted using 100 &#x003bc;M Cd. (B) Time course for effects of 1 &#x003bc;M &#x003c9;-Aga-IVA. Steady state Ba<sup>2+</sup> current elicited by depolarizations to either &#x02212;15 mV (wild type) or &#x02212;20 mV (both mutants) at 10-s intervals before and after application of toxin. Inward Ba<sup>2+</sup> currents were measured 10 ms after depolarization. (Same cells as in A.) (C) Plot of fraction of uninhibited (I<sup>n</sup>/I<sup>n</sup><sub>0</sub>) current against toxin concentration for wild type, Glu188Lys, and Glu1658Lys. I<sup>n</sup>/I<sup>n</sup><sub>0</sub> is the fraction of uninhibited current in the plateau phase at negative voltages, typically &#x02212;25 to &#x02212;5 mV (<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">Fig. 2</xref> C). Smooth curves are fits of I<sup>n</sup>/I<sup>n</sup><sub>0</sub> = 1 &#x02013; {[toxin]/([toxin] + <italic>K</italic><sub>d</sub>)} to the data, where <italic>K</italic><sub>d</sub> is the equilibrium dissociation constant for single toxin binding sites. <italic>K</italic><sub>d</sub> values are as follows: 160 nM for wild-type&#x0003b; 110 nM for Glu188Lys&#x0003b; and 33 &#x003bc;M for Glu1658Lys. Data are means &#x000b1; SEM for between three and eight oocytes at each toxin concentration. Data for wild type are from <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">Fig. 2</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8186.f4"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>